[=#keep_dense_rank_to_array_agg]
= keep_dense_rank_to_array_agg =

Oracle's //keep (dense_rank first|last order by ...)// clause on //max()// and //min()// picks the aggregated column's value from whichever row sorts first (or last) within each group.  !PostgreSQL has no //keep()// syntax, so the '''keep_dense_rank_to_array_agg''' module rewrites the call as an ordered //array_agg()// and takes its first element.

A query like:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
select max(pfn.pathfullname) keep (dense_rank last order by hv.versionobjid) pathfullname
from harversions hv, harpathfullname pfn
where pfn.versionobjid=hv.versionobjid
group by hv.itemobjid
}}}
}}}

Would be translated to:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
select (array_agg(pfn.pathfullname order by hv.versionobjid desc nulls first,
                   pfn.pathfullname desc nulls last))[1] pathfullname
from harversions hv, harpathfullname pfn
where pfn.versionobjid=hv.versionobjid
group by hv.itemobjid
}}}
}}}

An example configuration:

{{{#!blockquote
{{{#!code
@parts/sqlrelay-querytranslations-keep-dense-rank-to-array-agg.conf@
}}}
}}}

The module has no module-specific attributes - only the standard '''module''', '''enabled''', and '''debug''' attributes apply.

Note that in this example, the normalize query translation is loaded prior to the keep_dense_rank_to_array_agg query translation.  Pattern matching is substantially more reliable and efficient if the query has been normalized first.  See the [normalize.html#normalize normalize] query translation for more information.

== Considerations ==

Calls inside string literals are left alone.  Identifiers like //mymax(...)// that merely end in //max// or //min// are also left alone.

Only a single sort key is supported inside //order by//, and only a single argument is supported inside //max()// or //min()//; either case is passed through untranslated.  An analytic form, eg. //max(x) keep (dense_rank first order by y) over (...)//, also can't be expressed this way and is left untranslated.

The argument of //max()// or //min()// appears twice in the rewritten query, so an argument whose value changes each time it's evaluated (eg. a call to a random-number function) may give different results than on Oracle.

